  {
    id:'opqb-t28-24', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'All of the following conditions can result in amblyopia except:',
    options:['Congenital cataract of the right eye','Alternating esotropia','Left total congenital ptosis','Cigar smoking'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Amblyopia is a developmental failure of the visual cortex, and the book lists exactly what can cause it. Cigar smoking is not on that list, and could not be — it acts on an adult optic nerve, not on a developing visual system.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` **p.205** defines the disease first: *\"a **neurodevelopmental** visual disorder with **unilateral** (or less commonly, bilateral) **reduction of best-corrected visual acuity** that cannot be corrected by glasses **in an otherwise healthy eye**… caused by an **abnormal visual experience early in life**.\"* Two clauses do all the work — *early in life*, and *otherwise healthy eye*.\n\nThe same page prints the four **predisposing factors**:\n\n> 1. **Strabismus** (approximately half of the cases, mainly esotropia)\n> 2. **Anisometropia** (asymmetric refractive errors)\n> 3. **Form deprivation and occlusion amblyopia** (opacities in ocular media such as **cataract**, corneal scarring, or upper eyelid **ptosis**)\n> 4. **Bilateral uncorrected refractive errors** (amblyopia is more with high hypermetropia)\n\n**Three of the four options are on that list by name:**\n\n| Option | Which factor it is |\n|---|---|\n| **Congenital cataract of the right eye** | Factor 3, and the book's own first example of an opacity in the ocular media. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.207 makes the consequence explicit: *\"Visually significant cataract requires **immediate** treatment once diagnosed to avoid **amblyopia**\"*, and untreated unilateral congenital cataract goes on to **sensory squint**. |\n| **Alternating esotropia** | Factor 1. Esotropia is the book's named commonest strabismic cause. |\n| **Left total congenital ptosis** | Factor 3 again — *\"upper eyelid ptosis\"* is printed in the same parenthesis as cataract. A **total** ptosis occludes the pupil from birth, which is deprivation in its purest form. |\n\n**Why cigar smoking is the exception.** Tobacco does have an optic-nerve toxicity — the entity called **tobacco–alcohol amblyopia**, a bilateral, symmetrical, painless centrocaecal scotoma from a toxic/nutritional optic neuropathy. But it is a misnomer: there is *visible pathology in the pathway*, so it fails the book's own \"otherwise healthy eye\" clause, and it strikes adults long after the sensitive period the book caps at **8–10 years** (p.205: *\"amblyopia treatment is less successful after age 8-10 years\"*). It is an optic neuropathy wearing amblyopia's name. *(Tobacco–alcohol amblyopia is **not taken from the course material** — the cached ophthalmology decks and chapters name no toxic amblyopia. It is given because the bank offers the option and a reader will meet the term.)*\n\n**⚠️ A nuance the bank does not acknowledge, recorded so it is not a surprise in the exam.** Classical teaching holds that an **alternating** squint *spares* the eyes from amblyopia, because fixation swaps between them and neither is chronically suppressed — amblyopia is the penalty of a **unilateral** squint. On that reading option B would be a second defensible exception. The book does not draw the distinction: p.205 attributes amblyopia to \"strabismus\" without qualifying it, and ch.15 (p.198) says only that suppression develops *\"to overcome double vision\"*. **The bank's key stands and is not disputed** — cigar smoking is the more clearly correct exception, since it is not a developmental cause at all. *(The alternating-squint sparing is **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Anything that blurs, occludes or misaligns one eye in the first decade is an amblyogenic emergency; the same insult in an adult is not. That is why the congenital cataract in option A is operated *immediately* and a senile one is not.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: `op-cat` — two of the four options are lens and lid deprivation rather than squint.)*",
    objective:'Amblyopia has four predisposing factors in the book — strabismus, anisometropia, form deprivation (cataract, ptosis) and bilateral uncorrected refractive error — all acting before age 8–10; a toxic optic neuropathy in an adult is not one of them.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.170, Q24 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.205 (amblyopia definition, predisposing factors, 8–10 year limit), p.207 (visually significant congenital cataract must be treated immediately to avoid amblyopia); `ophthalmology.pdf` p.198 (suppression in strabismus).'
  },

  {
    id:'opqb-t28-25', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract-astig',
    stem:'In regular astigmatism, light rays focus into:',
    options:['Multiple points','A focal line in front of or behind the retina','Two focal lines','A focal line perpendicular to the retina'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The definition is the answer, and the deck flags it as high yield.** `L7,8) Refractive errors` slide 61:\n\n> **Astigmatism** — *\"It is a state of refraction in which parallel rays of light entering the eye **will not uniformly focus to a single point on the retina but instead into two focal lines**\"* (**a = no, stigma = point**). *\"It is due [to a] non-spherical surface of the **cornea (more common)** or the **lens**, with the resultant difference in the refractive power at **different meridians**.\"*\n\n**Why two lines and not one, and not a smear.** A regularly astigmatic surface is toric — steeper in one meridian, flatter in the meridian at right angles to it, and changing smoothly between them. The deck's own picture for this is the **football beside the soccer ball** (slide 62, labelled *NORMAL* / *ASTIGMATISM*), and slide 63 labels the two meridians **flatter direction** and **steeper direction**. The steep meridian brings its rays to a focus earlier, and because every ray in that meridian converges while the perpendicular meridian is still travelling, the image at that plane is not a point but a **line lying along the flat meridian**. Further back, the flat meridian focuses in its turn, giving a second line at right angles to the first. Between the two lies the interval containing the **circle of least confusion**, which is what the eye actually uses and why an uncorrected astigmat sees blur rather than doubling.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Whose description it is |\n|---|---|\n| **Multiple points** | **Irregular** astigmatism — the surface has no two clean principal meridians, so there is no orderly pair of line foci. `L7,8` slide 64 puts it on its own branch of the causes tree: *\"**Irregular (less common)** — **Corneal scars and Keratoconus**\"*. This is the trap that separates the two halves of the topic, and it is why irregular astigmatism cannot be corrected with a cylindrical lens. |\n| **A focal line in front of or behind the retina** | Half an answer. It correctly locates *one* line relative to the retina, which is how slide 66's five types are drawn (simple myopic, compound myopic, mixed, simple hyperopic, compound hyperopic) — but a single line is not a description of astigmatism, it is a description of one of its two meridians. A single point in front of or behind the retina would be simple myopia or hyperopia. |\n| **A focal line perpendicular to the retina** | Geometrically impossible as written. The focal lines of Sturm's conoid lie in planes **parallel** to the retina, at right angles **to each other**, not standing on end through it. |\n\n**What follows clinically.** Because the two meridians are fixed and orthogonal, regular astigmatism is fully correctable with a **cylinder** at the right axis — slide 64 subdivides it into **simple** (one meridian emmetropic), **compound** (both meridians myopic or both hyperopic) and **mixed** (one of each). Irregular astigmatism is not, and needs a rigid contact lens or corneal surgery instead. `opqb-t28-42` in this same exam is the other half of the topic: keratometry finding corneal astigmatism that the lens has already neutralised.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Regular astigmatism focuses parallel light into two focal lines rather than one point, because a toric surface has two orthogonal principal meridians of different power; multiple points is irregular astigmatism.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.170, Q25 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `L7,8) Refractive errors` sl.61 (definition — high yield), sl.62–63 (the two meridians), sl.64 (causes tree: lenticular vs corneal, regular vs irregular), sl.66 (the five types).'
  },

  {
    id:'opqb-t28-26', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'Iridodonesis is seen in all of the following conditions except:',
    options:['Aphakia','Shrunken hyper mature cataract','Subluxated lens','Iridodialysis'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Iridodonesis is a tremulous iris, and it happens for exactly one reason: the iris has lost the lens it normally rests against.** Iridodialysis is a torn iris ROOT — a different lesion with a different sign.\n\n**The mechanism.** The posterior surface of the iris lies on the anterior lens capsule, and the lens is what holds it steady. Remove the lens, shrink it, or let it fall back on loosened zonules, and the iris loses its backboard: it now flutters with every saccade, quivering visibly as the eye stops moving. `L18) Cataract II` prints this among the classic signs of **aphakia** — *deep anterior chamber*, *iridodonesis*, *jet-black pupil* — the triad that tells you at the slit lamp that no lens is present.\n\n**Why the first three all produce it:**\n\n| Option | Why the iris shakes |\n|---|---|\n| **Aphakia** | The lens is gone outright — surgically, or after an extracapsular loss. Nothing supports the iris at all, and `L18) Cataract II` lists iridodonesis among the classic signs. |\n| **Shrunken hyper mature cataract** | A **hypermature** (Morgagnian / shrunken) cataract has lost cortical water; the lens shrivels and its anterior capsule falls away from the iris. The iris is now unsupported over a lens that no longer fills its space, so it wobbles — the same physics as aphakia in a lesser degree. `L17) Cataract I` places hypermaturity at the end of the maturation sequence, and the shrunken sac is also why these eyes are at risk of **phacolytic** glaucoma and lens subluxation. |\n| **Subluxated lens** | The zonules are partly broken, so the lens is displaced and unsupported in the meridian where they gave way. That sector of iris tremulates and the anterior chamber depth becomes irregular. This is the classic sign hunted for in **Marfan syndrome**, homocystinuria and blunt trauma. |\n\n**Why iridodialysis is the exception.** Iridodialysis is a **disinsertion of the iris root from the ciliary body**, almost always from blunt trauma. What it produces is a **D-shaped pupil** and a black crescent at the limbus through which a second, peripheral \"pupil\" is visible — often with **monocular diplopia** or glare, and typically with hyphaema at the time of injury. The iris here is *torn away at its base*, not *unsupported behind*. The rest of the iris still rests on an intact lens, so it does not tremble. Different lesion, different sign — that is the whole point of the question.\n\n**⚠️ Printed as it appears.** Option B prints **\"hyper mature\"** as two words; \"hypermature\" is the standard single word. The stem and options are reproduced exactly as the bank set them.\n\n**Trap to avoid.** Both iridodonesis and iridodialysis begin *irido-*, both are iris signs, and both are classically traumatic. The discriminator is one word: *-donesis* is **trembling** (unsupported iris), *-dialysis* is **separation** (torn root).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: `op-trauma` — iridodialysis and lens subluxation are both blunt-trauma lesions.)*",
    objective:'Iridodonesis is a tremulous iris from loss of lens support — aphakia, a shrunken hypermature cataract, or a subluxated lens; iridodialysis is a torn iris root producing a D-shaped pupil instead.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.170, Q26 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `L18) Cataract II` (classic signs of aphakia — deep anterior chamber, iridodonesis, jet-black pupil); `L17) Cataract I` (maturation sequence to hypermature/Morgagnian).'
  },

  {
    id:'opqb-t28-27', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-pupil',
    stem:"Horner's syndrome is characterized by all of the following except:",
    options:['Miosis','Widened palpebral fissure','Slight enophthalmos','Unilateral absence of sweating'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Every sign of Horner's syndrome is a sign of something switched OFF, because the lesion is a loss of sympathetic supply. A widened palpebral fissure is a sign of sympathetic OVERactivity — it points the opposite way.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` **p.180** and `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` both give the tetrad of an oculosympathetic palsy:\n\n> **Ptosis** (mild, from paralysis of **Müller's muscle**) · **Miosis** (unopposed sphincter, from paralysis of the **dilator pupillae**) · **Apparent enophthalmos** · **Anhidrosis** of the ipsilateral face.\n\n**Sign by sign, and why three of the four are correct:**\n\n| Option | Which paralysed structure produces it |\n|---|---|\n| **Miosis** | The **dilator pupillae** is sympathetic. Lose it and the parasympathetic **sphincter** acts unopposed, so the pupil is small. The give-away at the bedside is **dilatation lag** — the affected pupil is slowest to widen in the dark, so the **anisocoria is greater in dim light**, the reverse of a third-nerve pupil. Light and near reactions stay normal, because the sphincter and its nerve are untouched. |\n| **Slight enophthalmos** | Printed by the book, and correctly hedged as slight. It is **apparent, not true** — the globe has not receded; the narrowed fissure between a drooping upper lid and a slightly elevated lower lid makes the eye *look* sunken. (The lower lid rises for the same reason the upper falls: its own inferior tarsal smooth muscle is sympathetic too — the sign is called **upside-down ptosis**.) |\n| **Unilateral absence of sweating** | **Anhidrosis**, from loss of the sudomotor sympathetic fibres to the face. Its extent localises the lesion: a **central or preganglionic** lesion gives hemifacial anhidrosis, while a **postganglionic** lesion above the carotid bifurcation may spare sweating altogether, because those fibres travel with the external carotid. |\n\n**Why a widened palpebral fissure is the exception.** The fissure in Horner's is **narrowed**, not widened — **Müller's muscle** (the superior tarsal muscle) is sympathetically innervated and contributes about 2 mm of lid elevation, so denervation drops the upper lid. The book records the ptosis explicitly as *mild* or *partial*, which is the second discriminator from a third-nerve palsy: **III** paralyses the *levator*, giving a **complete** ptosis with a **dilated** pupil; **Horner's** paralyses only *Müller's*, giving a **partial** ptosis with a **constricted** pupil.\n\nA **widened** fissure means the opposite physiology: **sympathetic overactivity or lid retraction** — thyroid eye disease (the commonest cause by far, with **von Graefe's** lid lag), a **contralateral** ptosis producing apparent retraction on the good side, or dorsal midbrain disease (**Collier's sign**). None of these belongs to an oculosympathetic palsy.\n\n**Why the question matters clinically.** A newly acquired Horner's is never cosmetic — the sympathetic chain runs a three-neurone course from hypothalamus to brainstem to lung apex to carotid, so the differential includes **Pancoast tumour**, **carotid dissection** (painful, with headache or neck pain — a stroke warning) and brainstem infarction. The three positive signs in this question are the ones that send the patient for imaging.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: `op-lid` — the ptosis is a lid sign and is the commonest reason these patients present.)*",
    objective:"Horner's syndrome is a loss of sympathetic supply: partial ptosis with a NARROWED fissure, miosis with dilatation lag, apparent enophthalmos and anhidrosis; a widened fissure indicates sympathetic overactivity or lid retraction instead.",
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.170, Q27 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.180 (Horner syndrome — ptosis from Müller\'s muscle, miosis, apparent enophthalmos, anhidrosis); `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` sl.11 (Horner\'s).'
  },

  {
    id:'opqb-t28-28', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'The following condition does not cause exposure keratitis:',
    options:['Facial nerve palsy','Proptosis',"Grave's disease",'Levator muscle palsy'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Exposure keratopathy needs a cornea that the lids cannot cover. A levator palsy makes the upper lid DROOP — it covers the cornea more, not less, so it protects rather than exposes.**\n\n`L15,16) Cornea` lists the causes of **Exposure Keratopathy** directly, and they are all mechanisms of **lagophthalmos** (failure of lid closure):\n\n> **Facial (VII) nerve palsy** · **Proptosis** (thyroid eye disease, orbital masses) · **lid deformities/scarring (cicatricial ectropion)** · **coma, anaesthesia or nocturnal lagophthalmos**\n\nThe damage is always in the same place — the **lower third or interpalpebral strip** of the cornea, which is the part left uncovered — beginning as punctate epithelial erosions, progressing to an epithelial defect, then to a sterile or secondarily infected ulcer, and in a neglected eye to perforation.\n\n**Why the first three all expose the cornea:**\n\n| Option | The mechanism of exposure |\n|---|---|\n| **Facial nerve palsy** | **Orbicularis oculi** is the closing muscle and it is supplied by **VII**. Paralyse it and the lid cannot shut — the textbook cause of paralytic lagophthalmos. `L5) Eyelid disease` sl.15–16 covers lagophthalmos on exactly this basis. The **Bell phenomenon** (the globe rolling up on attempted closure) is what decides whether the eye survives; when it is absent the cornea ulcerates fast. |\n| **Proptosis** | The globe is pushed forward beyond the reach of the lids, so the palpebral aperture cannot span it. Any orbital mass, cellulitis or haemorrhage does this. |\n| **Grave's disease** | Two mechanisms at once, which is why thyroid eye disease is the commonest cause seen in clinic: **proptosis** from expanded orbital fat and muscle, *plus* **upper lid retraction** from sympathetic overaction on Müller's muscle and fibrosis of levator. Widened fissure and forward globe together give the maximal exposed surface. |\n\n**Why levator palsy is the exception.** The **levator palpebrae superioris** is the *opening* muscle (III, with a sympathetic contribution from Müller's). Paralysing it produces **ptosis** — the upper lid sits lower and covers *more* cornea. Far from exposing the eye, a ptosis can be so protective that **tarsorrhaphy** or an induced ptosis (botulinum toxin to levator) is used deliberately as *treatment* for exposure keratopathy in a VII palsy. This is the pivot the question turns on: **orbicularis closes, levator opens** — only failure of the closer exposes.\n\n**⚠️ Two printings recorded, not repaired.** The stem prints **\"The following condition does not cause exposure keratitis\"** in the singular though four options follow; and option C prints **\"Grave's disease\"** with a singular possessive, where the eponym is **Graves' disease** (after Robert Graves — the s belongs to the name). Both are kept exactly as the bank set them.\n\n**One caveat worth carrying.** A ptosis protects only while it is *complete over the pupil and stable*. A **partial** ptosis with a coexisting VII palsy, or a levator palsy in an eye with a bad Bell phenomenon and reduced corneal sensation, can still dry out — the neurotrophic and exposure mechanisms compound. The bank's point stands, but the safe habit is to check closure and corneal sensation in every ptosis, not to assume the lid is doing its job.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: `op-lid` — every option is a lid or orbit mechanism acting on the cornea.)*",
    objective:'Exposure keratopathy follows lagophthalmos — VII palsy paralysing orbicularis, proptosis, or thyroid retraction; a levator palsy causes ptosis, which covers the cornea and is protective.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.170, Q28 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `L15,16) Cornea` (Exposure Keratopathy — causes and the lower-third/interpalpebral distribution); `L5) Eyelid disease` sl.15–16 (lagophthalmos).'
  },

  {
    id:'opqb-t28-29', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'The following are the clinical presentations of herpes simplex keratitis except:',
    options:['Superficial punctate keratitis','Dendritic keratitis','Central single ulcer with satellite lesions','Disciform keratitis'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**\"Satellite lesions\" is the single most reliable clinical word in corneal infection, and it means FUNGUS.** `L15,16) Cornea` prints it as the defining sign of **fungal keratitis**: a central ulcer with a dry, raised, greyish-white surface, **feathery indistinct margins**, and **satellite lesions** — small separate infiltrates lying around the main one, produced by hyphae spreading through the stroma beyond the visible edge. Nothing in the herpes simplex spectrum does this.\n\n**The three that ARE herpes simplex.** `L15,16) Cornea` sets HSV keratitis out as a spectrum by depth, and the exam wants the whole ladder:\n\n| Option | Where it sits in the HSV spectrum |\n|---|---|\n| **Superficial punctate keratitis** | The **earliest epithelial** stage. Live virus replicating in the epithelium gives scattered swollen cells that stain punctately before they coalesce — the vesicles that will become the dendrite. It is easy to dismiss as dry eye, which is exactly why the bank includes it. *(Naming SPK specifically as the earliest HSV presentation is **not taken from the course material** — the cached deck lists punctate epithelial keratitis by name under HZO and describes the HSV epithelial stage without that label. The staging as epithelial → dendritic → geographic → stromal is the deck's own.)* |\n| **Dendritic keratitis** | The pathognomonic sign and the **epithelial** hallmark: a branching linear ulcer with **terminal bulbs** and **swollen epithelial cells at its edges**, best seen with **fluorescein** (which pools in the bed) and **rose bengal** (which stains the virus-laden edge cells). Extend it with topical steroid or in an immunocompromised host and it becomes the **geographic** ulcer. |\n| **Disciform keratitis** | The **stromal/endothelial** stage, and an **immune** rather than an actively infectious lesion: a central disc of **stromal oedema** with **keratic precipitates** behind it, an intact epithelium, and no necrosis. Because it is immune, it is the one HSV lesion **treated with steroid**, always under antiviral cover — the reverse of the rule for the epithelial forms, where steroid is the classic disaster. |\n\n**Why the discriminator is worth memorising.** The clinical stakes are asymmetrical. A dendrite treated as fungal loses time; a **fungal ulcer treated with the steroid** you would give a disciform keratitis melts the cornea. So the sign hierarchy runs: **satellites, feathery margins, a dry raised slough, and a history of vegetable-matter trauma → fungal**; **branching ulcer with terminal bulbs and reduced corneal sensation → HSV**.\n\n**One more separator on the same page.** HSV also **reduces corneal sensation** — the deck notes it, and it is the reason recurrent disease drifts toward a **neurotrophic** ulcer with a smooth rolled edge and no infiltrate. A fungal ulcer leaves sensation intact. Testing the cornea with a wisp of cotton before instilling anaesthetic is a free bedside test that separates two of the four options here.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Herpes simplex keratitis runs superficial punctate → dendritic → geographic → disciform; a central ulcer with satellite lesions and feathery margins is fungal keratitis, and steroid separates the two managements.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.170, Q29 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `L15,16) Cornea` (fungal keratitis — satellite lesions, feathery margins; HSV keratitis — epithelial dendritic with terminal bulbs, disciform stromal form, reduced corneal sensation).'
  },

  {
    id:'opqb-t28-30', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'Band shaped keratopathy is seen in the:',
    options:['Upper part of the cornea','Interpalpebral part of the cornea','Lower part of the cornea','Peripheral parts of the cornea'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Band keratopathy is a deposit of calcium in Bowman's layer, and it lands in the interpalpebral strip because that is the part of the cornea the lids leave open.** `L15,16) Cornea` describes it as a **horizontal band of greyish-white opacity across the interpalpebral zone**, beginning at the **3 and 9 o'clock periphery** with a **clear zone separating it from the limbus**, then extending centrally until the two ends meet as a continuous band, classically peppered with small **holes** where nerves pierce Bowman's layer (the Swiss-cheese appearance).\n\n**Why the exposed strip and nowhere else.** The tear film over the open palpebral aperture **evaporates**, which concentrates solutes and raises local pH; **carbon dioxide is lost** from that surface, alkalinising the tissue further; and calcium phosphate is a salt whose solubility falls as pH rises. So the exposed band is where calcium precipitates first, and the lid-covered upper and lower cornea are spared. The lid position dictates the shape of the sign — the same reason the **clear zone at the limbus** appears, since limbal vessels keep that rim nourished.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — each is a different disease's territory:**\n\n| Option | What actually lives there |\n|---|---|\n| **Upper part of the cornea** | Superior limbic keratoconjunctivitis, superior pannus of **trachoma** (`L6) Conjunctiva` — pannus begins **superiorly** under the tarsal disease), and vernal limbal disease. |\n| **Lower part of the cornea** | **Exposure** keratopathy and **staphylococcal marginal** disease drift to the lower third; `opqb-t28-28` in this same exam is the exposure question. |\n| **Peripheral parts of the cornea** | **Arcus senilis** (lipid, with a clear zone at the limbus, in the periphery all around), Mooren's ulcer, and peripheral ulcerative keratitis. Arcus is the classic confusable, because it too spares a limbal rim — but arcus is **annular** and **lipid**, band keratopathy is **horizontal** and **calcium**. |\n\n**Why it appears at all.** The causes divide cleanly: **chronic ocular inflammation** (long-standing **uveitis**, above all **juvenile idiopathic arthritis-associated** anterior uveitis, and any **phthisical** eye), **hypercalcaemia** (hyperparathyroidism, sarcoidosis, vitamin D excess, milk-alkali), **chronic silicone oil** or drop toxicity, and hereditary forms. A band keratopathy in a child is a demand for a rheumatology and a serum-calcium referral, not just a corneal one. Treatment when it crosses the visual axis is **chelation with EDTA** after epithelial removal.\n\n**⚠️ Reprint — recorded, not folded.** This question is already in the corpus as `opqb-t8-322`: identical stem, identical four options in the identical order, identical key. Within-bank reprint of the topic section into the past paper. Written out here as instructed; a later sweep decides the fold.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Band keratopathy is calcium in Bowman's layer in the interpalpebral strip, with a clear limbal zone — driven by tear evaporation and CO2 loss over the exposed cornea, and caused by chronic uveitis, phthisis or hypercalcaemia.",
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.170, Q30 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `L15,16) Cornea` (band keratopathy — interpalpebral band, clear limbal zone, calcium in Bowman\'s layer); `L6) Conjunctiva` (superior pannus of trachoma, for contrast). Already in the corpus as `opqb-t8-322` (exact within-bank reprint, not folded here).'
  },

  {
    id:'opqb-t28-31', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'Regarding neovascular glaucoma:',
    options:['It starts as a secondary closed angle glaucoma','Can be easily controlled medically','Selective laser trabeculoplasty can lower the intraocular pressure','Pan retinal photocoagulation can prevent its occurrence'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**⚠️ The bank keys C. On the material as cached, C is false and D is the true statement. The key is recorded as printed and NOT moved, per house rule — but read the reasoning before you trust the letter.**\n\n**What neovascular glaucoma is.** `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` sets out the sequence: **retinal ischaemia** (proliferative diabetic retinopathy, **central retinal vein occlusion**, ocular ischaemic syndrome) releases **VEGF**, which diffuses forward and grows a **fibrovascular membrane** over the iris (**rubeosis iridis**) and across the **trabecular meshwork**. The membrane then **contracts**, dragging the peripheral iris up over the meshwork as **peripheral anterior synechiae**, and zipping the angle shut.\n\n**Option by option:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. It starts as a secondary closed angle glaucoma** | **False as written — it starts OPEN.** The deck's own two stages are the point: in the early stage the angle is still **open** and the pressure rises because the fibrovascular membrane **obstructs** the meshwork; only later does the membrane contract and **close** the angle by synechiae. So NVG is a secondary **open**-angle glaucoma that *becomes* a secondary **closed**-angle one. The word doing the damage is **starts**. |\n| **B. Can be easily controlled medically** | **False, and dangerously so.** NVG is among the most refractory glaucomas. Drops (aqueous suppressants — beta-blockers, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, alpha-agonists) and atropine plus steroid buy comfort and a little pressure, but **prostaglandins and miotics are avoided** in an inflamed eye, and once the angle is synechially closed no drop reaches a functioning outflow. Control usually needs a **tube shunt** or **cyclodestruction**, and many of these eyes end painful and blind. |\n| **C. Selective laser trabeculoplasty can lower the intraocular pressure — ⚠️ THE PRINTED KEY** | **False on the cached material.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` files **\"Laser trabeculoplasty (Argon, Selective)\"** under the treatment of **primary open angle glaucoma**, and SLT works by targeting pigmented trabecular cells in a meshwork that is *anatomically open and visible*. In NVG the meshwork is **covered by a fibrovascular membrane** and progressively **sealed by peripheral anterior synechiae** — there is no accessible meshwork to treat. Worse, lasering vascularised angle tissue invites **hyphaema** and more inflammation. Neovascular glaucoma is a standard **contraindication** to trabeculoplasty. |\n| **D. Pan retinal photocoagulation can prevent its occurrence** | **True, and it is the answer the material supports.** PRP ablates ischaemic peripheral retina, cuts VEGF production, and causes rubeosis to regress; given **before** the angle closes it prevents NVG outright, which is exactly why an ischaemic CRVO or proliferative diabetic retinopathy is photocoagulated urgently and why these eyes are examined with **gonioscopy and undilated iris inspection at every visit**. (Anti-VEGF injection does the same faster but more briefly, and is used to quiet the eye before surgery.) |\n\n**The recorded contradiction.** Two options, C and D, cannot both be the single answer, and the material supports D. The most likely origin is a mis-set answer line rather than a considered position — but **a defective key is noted, never disputed**, so `answer` stays at the printed **C**. Answer the exam with what the bank prints; carry D as what is true.\n\n**Practical rule.** NVG is prevented, not treated. Once the pupil margin shows a fringe of vessels, the clock has already started.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: `op-ret-dr` — the disease is retinal ischaemia expressing itself in the anterior segment.)*",
    objective:'Neovascular glaucoma begins as a secondary OPEN angle glaucoma from a fibrovascular membrane over the meshwork, becomes closed by synechiae, resists medical treatment, contraindicates trabeculoplasty, and is prevented by panretinal photocoagulation.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.171, Q31 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` (neovascular glaucoma — rubeosis, fibrovascular membrane over the trabeculum, contraction pulling iris across the angle); `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` (laser trabeculoplasty, Argon and Selective, filed under POAG treatment).'
  },

  {
    id:'opqb-t28-32', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'Signs of chronic angle closure glaucoma can include all of the following EXCEPT:',
    options:['Shallow anterior chamber','Constricted field','Hyperemia of optic disc','D. Peripheral anterior synechiac'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**A chronically glaucomatous disc is PALE and CUPPED, not hyperaemic. Disc hyperaemia is a sign of an inflamed or swollen nerve head — papillitis, papilloedema — which is the opposite pathology.**\n\n`L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` draws chronic angle closure as the quiet, creeping form of PACG — the eye that never had a dramatic acute attack, or had subacute ones that resolved, while **peripheral anterior synechiae** accumulated and the angle closed a sector at a time. The clinical picture it prints is therefore **a POAG-like picture on a closed angle**: raised pressure, progressive **cupping**, and progressive **field loss**, in a **white, quiet, painless** eye — which is precisely why it is so often found late.\n\n**Why the other three are signs of the disease:**\n\n| Option | Why it is present |\n|---|---|\n| **Shallow anterior chamber** | The anatomical predisposition and the finding that separates angle closure from open angle disease at a glance. The at-risk eye is **hypermetropic, short, with a large lens and a shallow chamber**; the shallow chamber is what allows iridotrabecular contact in the first place. Gonioscopy is the confirmation, and Van Herick at the slit lamp is the screen. |\n| **Constricted field** | The end result of chronic optic-nerve damage. Glaucomatous loss runs **arcuate/nasal step → advancing arcuate → tunnel vision with a temporal island** — and the deck's `L11` triad for the diagnosis of any glaucoma is **raised IOP + optic disc changes + visual field changes**. Chronic ACG loses field exactly as POAG does. |\n| **D. Peripheral anterior synechiac** | These are the *mechanism*, and finding them on gonioscopy is what makes the diagnosis **chronic** rather than primary open angle. Repeated appositional closure lets the peripheral iris adhere to the trabecular meshwork; each episode adds permanent, irreversible angle closure. |\n\n**Why disc hyperaemia is the exception.** Glaucoma kills axons; it does not inflame them. The chronic disc shows **enlarged cup-to-disc ratio, thinning of the neuroretinal rim (inferior and superior first — the ISNT rule broken), a bayoneting of vessels, nasal displacement of the vascular trunk, and pallor of the remaining rim**. A **hyperaemic** disc — swollen, with blurred margins, dilated surface capillaries and obscured vessels — belongs to **optic neuritis/papillitis, papilloedema, anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy or ocular inflammation**. Seeing it in a case labelled chronic glaucoma means the label is wrong.\n\n**⚠️ Printed defect, kept exactly as set.** Option D prints its **letter twice** — the line on the page reads **\"D.  D. Peripheral anterior synechiac\"**, verified at high magnification. The duplicated **\"D.\"** is kept *inside* the option text because that is where the bank printed it, and **\"synechiac\"** is kept as printed for **synechiae**. Neither is repaired in the stem or options; both are recorded here. The doubled letter does not shift any key: the options are still four, still in the printed order A–D, and the key **C** still points at *Hyperemia of optic disc*.\n\n**Bedside consequence.** In an eye with a shallow chamber and a cupped disc, the pressure alone will not tell you which glaucoma you are treating — **gonioscopy is the examination that decides**, and it changes management completely: a chronically closed angle needs **laser peripheral iridotomy** (and often lens extraction) before or alongside pressure-lowering drops, where POAG does not.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: `op-neuro-optic` — the discriminator is a disc appearance, hyperaemia versus pallor and cupping.)*",
    objective:'Chronic angle closure glaucoma shows a shallow chamber, peripheral anterior synechiae on gonioscopy, cupping and constricted fields in a white quiet eye; a hyperaemic disc indicates inflammation or swelling, not chronic glaucoma.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.171, Q32 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` (chronic angle closure — accumulating peripheral anterior synechiae, cupping and field loss in a quiet eye); `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` (the diagnostic triad: raised IOP, disc changes, field changes).'
  },

  {
    id:'opqb-t28-33', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'In congenital and developmental cataract:',
    options:['Blue dot cataract affects vision','Anterior polar cataract affects vision more than posterior polar cataract','Lamellar cataract is the most common type'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**⚠️ THREE options only.** This question prints **A, B and C** and nothing else — verified off the page image, where the space below C is blank. There is no option D. The printed key **C** is in range.\n\n**Why C is right.** `ophthalmology.pdf` **p.207** tabulates the morphological types of congenital and developmental cataract, and **lamellar (zonular) cataract is the commonest**. It is a discrete opaque **shell (lamella) surrounding a clear nucleus**, with clear cortex outside it, often carrying **riders** — spoke-like opacities straddling the equator of the affected zone. Because a whole layer sits in the visual axis, it is typically **bilateral, symmetrical and visually significant**, and it is the classic type produced by a transient insult during lens development (`L17) Cataract I` groups congenital/developmental cataract by the timing and type of the insult).\n\n**Why A is wrong.** **Blue dot cataract** (cataracta punctata caerulea) is the archetypal **visually insignificant** opacity: small punctate bluish dots scattered in the cortex and nucleus, extremely common, usually **found incidentally** in an otherwise normal eye, **non-progressive**, and **not** an indication for surgery. The book's table places it opposite lamellar for exactly this contrast — one you watch, one you operate. Stating that it *affects vision* inverts the teaching point.\n\n**Why B is wrong.** The pole comparison runs the **other** way. **Posterior** opacities cost more vision than **anterior** ones of the same size, for two reasons: the posterior lens sits closer to the **nodal point** of the eye, so an opacity there casts a proportionally larger and denser shadow on the retina; and it is worst **in bright light**, when the pupil constricts down onto it. This is the same optics that makes an acquired **posterior subcapsular** cataract so symptomatic — glare, poor near vision, difficulty in sunlight — while a denser-looking anterior cortical change is tolerated. An **anterior polar** cataract, by contrast, is usually a small, stationary, dense white dot on the anterior capsule that is frequently **visually insignificant**; it may be associated with **persistent pupillary membrane** and needs watching for anisometropic amblyopia rather than surgery. *(The nodal-point reasoning for why posterior opacities cost more vision is **not taken from the course material** — the book states the fact in its table without giving the optics.)*\n\n**The rule the question is really testing.** In a child, the question is never *what shape is it* but **is it visually significant** — and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.207 answers it: an opacity **central, dense and larger than about 3 mm** is significant, and *\"visually significant cataract requires **immediate** treatment once diagnosed to avoid amblyopia.\"* That deadline is the reason these morphologies are taught at all. `opqb-t28-24` in this same exam is the amblyopia side of the same coin.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: `op-cat-surg` — the classification exists to decide which of these children go to theatre.)*",
    objective:'Lamellar (zonular) cataract is the commonest congenital/developmental type and is visually significant; blue dot cataract is an incidental non-progressive opacity, and posterior polar opacities cost more vision than anterior ones.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.171, Q33 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.207 (blue dot versus lamellar table; visually significant cataract = central, dense, >3 mm, treat immediately to avoid amblyopia); `L17) Cataract I` (congenital and developmental types).'
  },

  {
    id:'opqb-t28-34', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'Staphylococcal infections in ophthalmology do nor include:',
    options:['Blepharitis','Corneal ulcer','Giant papillary conjunctivitis','Endophthalmitis'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Giant papillary conjunctivitis is a MECHANICAL and ALLERGIC reaction, not an infection. Nothing is growing on that tarsus — something is rubbing on it.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` **p.86** lists the causes of **giant papillae** and they are all non-infective: **vernal keratoconjunctivitis**, **contact lens wear**, **ocular prostheses**, and **exposed sutures or any foreign body** in chronic contact with the upper tarsal conjunctiva. The pathology is a chronic mechanical irritant driving a **type I / type IV hypersensitivity** in the tarsal conjunctiva; the papillae are fibrovascular tufts, each with its **own central vessel**, growing large enough (>1 mm) to be called cobblestones. Treatment is to **remove the stimulus** — stop or change the lens, bury or remove the suture, polish the prosthesis — with mast-cell stabilisers as support. An antibiotic does nothing.\n\n**Why the other three are staphylococcal by textbook:**\n\n| Option | The staphylococcal disease |\n|---|---|\n| **Blepharitis** | The prototype. **Anterior blepharitis** is *staphylococcal* by definition — crusts and collarettes at the lash bases, madarosis, trichiasis, and recurrent **styes** (external hordeolum, a staphylococcal abscess of Zeis/Moll). It also drives **marginal keratitis**: `L15,16) Cornea` describes the peripheral infiltrate as a **hypersensitivity to staphylococcal antigen** from the lid margin, sitting with a **clear zone from the limbus** — the sterile companion lesion. |\n| **Corneal ulcer** | *Staphylococcus aureus* and *S. epidermidis* are among the commonest organisms in bacterial keratitis — a suppurative ulcer with a dense white infiltrate, sharply defined edges, hypopyon and marked pain, following a break in the epithelium. |\n| **Endophthalmitis** | **Coagulase-negative staphylococci (*S. epidermidis*) are the single commonest organism in acute post-operative endophthalmitis**, seeded from the patient's own lid and lash flora at surgery — which is exactly why povidone-iodine to the lids and fornices before cataract surgery is the one prophylactic step with the strongest evidence. *S. aureus* causes a more fulminant, more destructive form. |\n\n**The pattern worth taking away.** Staphylococcus reaches the eye from the **lid margin**, and it causes disease at three levels — on the lid (blepharitis, stye), on the cornea (ulcer, and the sterile marginal hypersensitivity), and inside the eye when the barrier is breached (endophthalmitis). Giant papillary conjunctivitis sits outside that pattern entirely, on the *upper tarsal* surface, caused by contact rather than colonisation.\n\n**⚠️ Printed defect, kept as set.** The stem prints **\"do nor include\"** for *do not include* — verified at high magnification. Reproduced exactly as the bank printed it; the meaning is unaffected and no option changes.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: `op-lid` — blepharitis and the lid-margin reservoir tie three of the four options together.)*",
    objective:'Staphylococcus causes blepharitis, bacterial corneal ulcer and post-operative endophthalmitis from the lid-margin flora; giant papillary conjunctivitis is a mechanical/allergic tarsal reaction to lenses, prostheses or sutures.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.171, Q34 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.86 (causes of giant papillae — vernal, contact lenses, prostheses, exposed sutures); `L15,16) Cornea` (marginal keratitis as staphylococcal antigen hypersensitivity from the lid margin).'
  },

  {
    id:'opqb-t28-35', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'Considering the management of ptosis:',
    options:['In paralytic ptosis, surgery should correct the ptosis before the strabismus','All congenital unilateral ptosis cases should be operated upon immediately','Mechanical ptosis necessitates a levator resection surgery','Frontalis suspension is needed if the levator function is absent'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The whole of ptosis surgery is chosen by ONE measurement: levator function — the lid excursion from full downgaze to full upgaze with the brow held.** `L5) Eyelid disease` gives the ladder, and option D is its bottom rung.\n\n| Levator function | Operation |\n|---|---|\n| **Good, >8 mm** | **Levator resection** (or a posterior Müller's-muscle/conjunctival resection for a small, phenylephrine-responsive ptosis) — there is a working muscle to shorten. |\n| **Fair, 5–7 mm** | **Large levator resection**. |\n| **Poor or absent, <4 mm** | **Frontalis suspension (sling)** — the lid is slung to the brow with fascia lata or silicone so the **frontalis** lifts it, because shortening a muscle that does not contract achieves nothing. |\n\nThat is why **D is true**: with **absent** levator function there is no useful excursion to gain from resection, and the only remaining lifter is the frontalis.\n\n**Why the other three are false:**\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Correct the ptosis before the strabismus** | **Reversed.** In paralytic ptosis — most often a **third nerve palsy** — the **squint is corrected first**, then the ptosis. Two reasons, both serious. First, the vertical squint changes the lid position, so operating the lid first means operating it twice. Second and decisively, **the ptosis is protecting the cornea**: a III palsy leaves a large-angle exotropia with a paralysed medial rectus and often a poor Bell phenomenon, so lifting the lid over a misaligned eye buys **intractable diplopia** and exposes an eye that may not close properly. The lid is the last thing you touch. |\n| **B. All congenital unilateral ptosis operated immediately** | **False — the indication is amblyopia, not the ptosis.** Surgery is urgent only if the lid **covers the visual axis** (or forces a chin-up head posture), because then it is a form-deprivation amblyogen — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.205 lists *upper eyelid ptosis* among the deprivation causes. A ptosis clear of the axis is deliberately **deferred to age 3–5**, when levator function can be measured reliably and the result will last. Operating every case immediately over-treats the majority and measures the muscle badly. |\n| **C. Mechanical ptosis necessitates a levator resection** | **False — treat the cause, not the lid.** Mechanical ptosis is a lid weighed down or tethered by something: a **chalazion**, a lid **tumour**, **oedema**, **scarring**, or **dermatochalasis**. Excise the lesion or release the scar and the lid rises on its own levator, which was never weak. Resecting a normal levator on top of an unremoved mass produces a mess. |\n\n**The measurement that makes the answer safe.** Before any of this: measure **MRD1**, **palpebral fissure height**, **levator function**, check for **Bell's phenomenon** and **corneal sensation**, and exclude the medical causes that must never be cut — **myasthenia gravis** (variable, fatigable ptosis, Cogan's lid twitch), **Horner's syndrome** (`opqb-t28-27` in this exam — partial ptosis with miosis), and progressive external ophthalmoplegia.\n\n**⚠️ Reprint — recorded, not folded.** Already in the corpus as `opqb-t5-181` with the same stem, the same option order and the same key; option D there reads *\"**The** frontalis suspension is needed…\"* where this printing reads *\"Frontalis suspension is needed…\"* — a respelling only. Written out as instructed; a later sweep decides the fold.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: `op-squint` — option A is a squint-sequencing rule.)*",
    objective:'Ptosis surgery is chosen by levator function: resection when it is good, frontalis suspension when it is absent; squint is corrected before paralytic ptosis, congenital ptosis is operated urgently only when it occludes the axis, and mechanical ptosis is treated by removing the cause.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.171, Q35 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `L5) Eyelid disease` (ptosis — levator function grading and the choice of resection versus frontalis suspension); `ophthalmology.pdf` p.205 (upper eyelid ptosis as a deprivation amblyogen). Already in the corpus as `opqb-t5-181` (within-bank reprint, option D respelled, not folded here).'
  },

  {
    id:'opqb-t28-36', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'Applanation tonometry in a case of chronic open angle glaucoma:',
    options:['Can be performed using a Schiotz tonometer','Must be performed on a sitting patient','Is inaccurate on eyes with abnormal scleral rigidity','Is complementary to perimetry'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**⚠️ The bank keys C. Scleral (ocular) rigidity is the limitation of the SCHIOTZ indentation tonometer, and the great advantage of Goldmann applanation is that it is largely INDEPENDENT of it. On the material as cached, C is false and D is true. The key is recorded as printed and NOT moved.**\n\n**The physics the question turns on.** `ophthalmology.pdf` **pp.32–33** sets the two methods against each other.\n\n- **Indentation (Schiotz)** presses a plunger into the cornea and *displaces a volume* of aqueous. How far it sinks depends not only on the pressure but on how distensible the coats of that particular eye are — the book's own wording is that **a firmer eye gives a lower indentation** for the same true pressure. So **ocular rigidity is built into the reading**, and Schiotz over-reads in a low-rigidity eye (high myope, post-refractive surgery) and under-reads in a rigid one.\n- **Applanation (Goldmann)** does the opposite: it **flattens a fixed small area** (3.06 mm diameter) rather than indenting, displacing a negligible volume — about 0.5 µl. Because almost no volume moves, the distensibility of the sclera barely enters the calculation. That is precisely *why* Goldmann displaced Schiotz and is the reference standard.\n\n**Option by option:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Can be performed using a Schiotz tonometer** | **False by definition.** Schiotz is an **indentation** tonometer, not an applanation one. The question names applanation; the instruments are Goldmann (slit-lamp mounted), Perkins (hand-held) and Tono-Pen/pneumotonometer by extension. |\n| **B. Must be performed on a sitting patient** | **False.** Goldmann is slit-lamp mounted and so is used sitting in practice, but *must* is wrong: **Perkins** is a hand-held applanation tonometer designed for the **supine, anaesthetised, bed-bound or paediatric** patient, and gives applanation readings in any posture. (Note also that pressure genuinely rises a few mmHg on lying flat — a real effect, not an instrument error.) |\n| **C. Is inaccurate on eyes with abnormal scleral rigidity — ⚠️ THE PRINTED KEY** | **The Schiotz limitation, not the Goldmann one.** Applanation *is* affected by other corneal properties — **central corneal thickness** (thin corneas under-read, thick over-read), **corneal oedema**, **scarring** and **high astigmatism** — and it is fair to say applanation is not perfectly independent of the eye's mechanics. But *scleral rigidity* is the classical Schiotz error, and stating it of applanation reverses the teaching point. *(That applanation is largely independent of ocular rigidity, and the specific role of central corneal thickness, is **not taken from the course material** — the cached chapter contrasts indentation and applanation without naming CCT.)* |\n| **D. Is complementary to perimetry** | **True, and the statement the material supports.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` builds the diagnosis on a **triad — intraocular pressure, optic disc changes, and visual field changes** — no one of which is sufficient. Pressure alone misses **normal-tension glaucoma** and over-calls **ocular hypertension** (`opqb-t28-40` in this exam is exactly that trap: raised pressure with a normal nerve and normal fields is *not* glaucoma). Fields alone cannot tell you what to treat or measure the response. Tonometry and perimetry are complementary by design: one measures the risk factor you can lower, the other measures the damage you are trying to prevent. |\n\n**The recorded contradiction.** Two options cannot both be single answers, and the material supports **D**. `answer` stays at the printed **C** — a defective key is noted, never disputed. Answer the exam with C; carry D as the truth.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: `op-intro-exam` — the question is really about how the instrument works.)*",
    objective:'Goldmann applanation flattens a fixed area and is largely independent of ocular rigidity (the Schiotz indentation error); it is used with perimetry and disc assessment as the three-part glaucoma diagnosis, and Perkins allows applanation supine.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.171, Q36 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `ophthalmology.pdf` pp.32–33 (tonometry — Schiotz indentation, a firmer eye gives a lower indentation; Goldmann applanation of a fixed area); `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` (the diagnostic triad: IOP, disc, fields).'
  },

  {
    id:'opqb-t28-37', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A clear zone between the lesion and the limbus is not present in:',
    options:['Arcus senilis','Catarrhal corneal ulcer','Trachomatous pannus',"Fleischer's ring"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**⚠️ The bank keys B here, but the SAME BANK keys C for the same question at `opqb-t6-244`, and the material supports C. The key is recorded as printed and NOT moved — read the reasoning before trusting the letter.**\n\n**The sign being tested.** A **clear zone** (lucid interval) between a peripheral corneal lesion and the limbus tells you the lesion is *not* fed continuously from the limbal vessels — it is either a deposit laid down short of the limbus, or an immune infiltrate seeded across a gap. A lesion **without** a clear zone is one that has **grown out of the limbus itself**, carrying vessels with it.\n\n| Option | Clear zone? | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Arcus senilis** | **Present** — the classic. A lipid deposit forming a grey-white ring in the peripheral stroma with a sharp outer edge and a **narrow clear lucid interval separating it from the limbus**. Age-related, and in a patient under 40 (arcus juvenilis) a prompt for a lipid profile. |\n| **B. Catarrhal (marginal) corneal ulcer — ⚠️ THE PRINTED KEY** | **Present, on the material.** `L15,16) Cornea` describes the marginal/catarrhal infiltrate as a **hypersensitivity to staphylococcal antigen** from the lid margin: the antigen diffuses in from the limbal vessels, meets antibody, and precipitates as a **separate infiltrate lying parallel to the limbus with a clear zone between the two** — the immune complexes deposit where the concentrations cross, short of the limbus. That lucid interval is the textbook sign that separates a marginal keratitis from an infective peripheral ulcer, and it is the standard exam pairing with arcus. |\n| **C. Trachomatous pannus** | **ABSENT — this is the lesion with no clear zone.** `L6) Conjunctiva` sl.28–29 and `ophthalmology.pdf` **p.92** describe pannus as **fibrovascular tissue growing from the limbus onto the cornea**, beginning **superiorly**, beneath the epithelium. The vessels are *continuous with the limbal arcade* — they cross the limbus and run on, by definition. There is no gap, because the lesion *is* an extension of the limbus. |\n| **D. Fleischer's ring** | **Present-ish, and in any case not a limbal lesion.** An **iron line at the base of the cone in keratoconus**, encircling the cone in the *central/paracentral* cornea and best seen in **cobalt blue**. It is nowhere near the limbus, so there is a wide clear zone by geography. |\n\n**The contradiction, recorded.** The corpus already holds this question as `opqb-t6-244` — **identical stem, identical four options in identical order** — keyed **C (trachomatous pannus)**. This printing keys **B**. Both cannot be right, and the definition of pannus (limbus-derived vessels crossing onto the cornea) settles it: **C is the medically correct answer.** House rule stands — `answer` here stays at the printed **B**, the discrepancy is recorded rather than repaired, and the fold decision belongs to a later sweep, not to this draft.\n\n**Practical use.** \"Is there a gap at the limbus?\" is a three-second slit-lamp question that sorts peripheral corneal disease fast: **gap → deposit or immune (arcus, marginal keratitis)**; **no gap → vascular ingrowth from the limbus (pannus, and by the same logic pterygium)**.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: `op-cornea` — three of the four options are corneal lesions.)*",
    objective:'A clear limbal zone marks a deposit or immune infiltrate (arcus, marginal/catarrhal ulcer, Fleischer ring); trachomatous pannus grows out of the limbus as fibrovascular tissue and therefore has no clear zone.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.171, Q37 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `L6) Conjunctiva` sl.28–29 (trachomatous pannus — fibrovascular ingrowth from the superior limbus); `ophthalmology.pdf` p.92 (pannus in active trachoma); `L15,16) Cornea` (marginal keratitis — staphylococcal antigen hypersensitivity with a clear zone from the limbus). Already in the corpus as `opqb-t6-244`, same options in the same order but keyed C — recorded, not folded.'
  },

  {
    id:'opqb-t28-38', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'Causes of a localized conjunctival redness include:',
    options:['Acute viral conjunctivitis','Phlyctenular keratoconjunctivitis','Mucopurulent conjunctivitis','Hay fever'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The word doing all the work is LOCALIZED. Conjunctivitis of any cause reddens the whole conjunctiva; a phlycten is a single nodule with a leash of vessels running to it, and nothing else.**\n\n`L6) Conjunctiva` sl.46–49 describes **phlyctenular keratoconjunctivitis** as a **delayed (type IV) hypersensitivity** to a microbial antigen — classically **tuberculoprotein**, and in current practice **staphylococcal** antigen from the lid margin. What it produces is a **small pinkish-white raised nodule at or near the limbus**, with **engorged conjunctival vessels converging on it** and normal conjunctiva elsewhere. That leash of vessels running to a discrete lump *is* localised redness. The nodule ulcerates, heals in one to two weeks, and can migrate onto the cornea as a **fascicular ulcer**, dragging its vessel bundle behind it and leaving a scar. Because the antigen has to come from somewhere, a phlycten is a prompt to examine the lid margins and — in an endemic setting — to think about **tuberculosis**.\n\n**Why each of the other three is diffuse:**\n\n| Option | Pattern of redness |\n|---|---|\n| **Acute viral conjunctivitis** | **Diffuse**, typically bilateral (second eye a few days behind the first), with **watery discharge**, a **follicular** tarsal reaction and a tender **preauricular node**. Adenoviral disease is highly contagious and reddens the whole conjunctival sac, fornices included. |\n| **Mucopurulent conjunctivitis** | **Diffuse** and bacterial — the redness is maximal in the **fornices** and fades toward the limbus, with **mucopurulent discharge** and lids stuck together on waking. That fornix-maximal, limbus-sparing pattern is the classic *conjunctival* injection, and it is the counterpart of the **circumcorneal (ciliary) injection** of keratitis, iritis and acute glaucoma. |\n| **Hay fever** | **Diffuse** and bilateral, an IgE-mediated seasonal allergic conjunctivitis — **itching** as the cardinal symptom, **chemosis**, watery discharge, and a papillary reaction over the whole tarsus. Allergy is never localised, because the allergen bathes the entire surface. |\n\n**The general rule to carry.** Localised conjunctival redness has a short differential, and each item is a discrete lesion rather than a surface inflammation: **phlycten**, **episcleritis** (a sectoral, mobile, blanching-with-phenylephrine patch), **scleritis** (sectoral, deep, violaceous, exquisitely tender, not blanching), a **subconjunctival haemorrhage**, a **foreign body**, an inflamed **pinguecula** or **pterygium**. Anything ending in *-itis* of the conjunctiva itself is diffuse.\n\n**Why the distinction earns its place in an exam.** Diffuse redness with discharge is treated topically and largely settles on its own; a *localised* red eye demands that you name the lump. Missing a scleritis behind an assumed conjunctivitis is the classic and sight-threatening version of that error.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: `op-red` — the question is a pattern-of-redness discriminator.)*",
    objective:'Localised conjunctival redness means a discrete lesion — a phlycten with its leash of vessels, episcleritis, scleritis, haemorrhage or foreign body; viral, bacterial and allergic conjunctivitis are all diffuse.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.171, Q38 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `L6) Conjunctiva` sl.46–49 (phlyctenular keratoconjunctivitis — type IV hypersensitivity, limbal nodule with converging vessels, fascicular ulcer).'
  },

  {
    id:'opqb-t28-39', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'Cover uncover test can be of benefit in the following conditions except:',
    options:['Diagnosis of esotropia','Differentiation between unilateral and alternating strabismus','Diagnosis of suppression','Differentiation between true and false strabismus'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**⚠️ The bank keys D. On the material as cached, D is a real and standard use of the cover test, and C is the one thing the test cannot do. The key is recorded as printed and NOT moved.**\n\n**What the cover test is.** `ophthalmology.pdf` **p.203** sets it out as a *motor* test — the patient fixates a target and the examiner watches for **movement**. Cover one eye: if the **uncovered** eye moves to take up fixation, there is a **manifest** deviation (tropia), and the direction of that movement names it — moving **out** to fixate means the eye was **in**, i.e. **esotropia**. Uncover and watch the **covered** eye: movement on uncovering reveals a **latent** deviation (phoria). Everything the test reports is a movement of an eye. It cannot report a *sensory* state.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Diagnosis of esotropia** | **A use.** The core function — a manifest convergent squint is diagnosed by the outward refixation movement of the uncovered eye. |\n| **B. Differentiation between unilateral and alternating strabismus** | **A use, and the book's own wording.** p.203 uses the **cover–uncover** test to establish whether one eye always takes up fixation (**unilateral**, the eye that fixates is dominant and the other is at risk of amblyopia) or whether either eye will hold fixation once uncovered (**alternating**). This distinction is exactly why it matters clinically: unilateral squint sends you looking for amblyopia. |\n| **C. Diagnosis of suppression — ⚠️ the true exception** | **NOT a use.** Suppression is a **sensory** adaptation — the cortex ignores the image from the deviating eye to avoid diplopia and confusion. No amount of covering and uncovering demonstrates it, because nothing about it moves. Suppression is tested by **sensory** tests: the **Worth four-dot test**, the **synoptophore**, **Bagolini striated glasses**, the 4-prism-dioptre base-out test. `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.15 does not print those tests at all — the chapter covers alignment and motility and stops there. *(That suppression is demonstrated by Worth four-dot, synoptophore or Bagolini glasses is **not taken from the course material** — the cached squint chapter names none of them; the point that a motor test cannot show a sensory phenomenon stands on the book's own description of what the cover test measures.)* |\n| **D. Differentiation between true and false strabismus — ⚠️ THE PRINTED KEY** | **This IS a use, and the standard one.** `ophthalmology.pdf` **p.197** describes **pseudostrabismus** — the eyes *look* crossed because of a **broad nasal bridge, prominent epicanthic folds** or an abnormal **angle kappa**, while the **visual axes are truly aligned**. The test that proves it is the **cover test**: in pseudostrabismus **no eye moves**, because there is nothing to correct; in a true squint the uncovered eye refixates. Saying it cannot do this contradicts the reason the test is done in every squint clinic in the first place. |\n\n**The recorded contradiction.** C and D cannot both be exceptions, and the material makes D a use and C an impossibility. `answer` stays at the printed **D** — a defective key is noted, never disputed. Answer the exam with D; carry C as the medically correct exception.\n\n**One practical caveat, in the bank's defence.** The cover test alone cannot *exclude* pseudostrabismus in an infant who will not fixate, and a **small-angle** squint (under about 10 prism dioptres) can hide within the noise, which is why the **corneal reflex (Hirschberg)** test is done alongside it. That is a limitation of sensitivity, not a statement that the test has no role — and it does not make D the exception the bank needs.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The cover test is a motor test: it diagnoses manifest deviations, separates unilateral from alternating squint, and distinguishes true strabismus from pseudostrabismus; suppression is sensory and needs Worth four-dot, synoptophore or Bagolini glasses instead.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.171, Q39 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.203 (cover and cover–uncover tests — manifest versus latent, unilateral versus alternating); p.197 (pseudostrabismus — epicanthic folds, broad nasal bridge, aligned visual axes).'
  },

  {
    id:'opqb-t28-40', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
    stem:'The following disease is not associated with field changes:',
    options:['Chronic angle closure glaucoma','Injury of the lateral jeniculate ate body','Retinitis pigmentosa','Ocular hypertension'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Ocular hypertension is DEFINED by the absence of damage. The moment a field defect appears, the diagnosis changes to glaucoma.**\n\n`L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` states it directly: **ocular hypertension is a raised intraocular pressure with a NORMAL optic nerve head and a NORMAL visual field, and an open angle.** It is a **risk factor**, not a disease of the visual pathway — a population of eyes under pressure, a minority of which will convert to glaucoma over years. That is exactly why these patients are **monitored** with serial discs, OCT and perimetry rather than automatically treated, and why the deck insists on the **triad — IOP + disc + fields** — for the diagnosis of glaucoma itself (`opqb-t28-36` in this exam is the other half of that point). Raised pressure alone is not glaucoma; normal pressure with disc and field damage (**normal-tension glaucoma**) is.\n\n**Why the other three all take field:**\n\n| Option | The field defect |\n|---|---|\n| **Chronic angle closure glaucoma** | Optic-nerve damage from chronically raised pressure produces the glaucomatous sequence: **paracentral scotoma and nasal step → arcuate (Bjerrum) scotoma → ring scotoma → tunnel vision with a spared temporal island**, central acuity preserved until late. `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` gives cupping and field loss as the picture of the chronic form, and `opqb-t28-32` in this exam covers its signs. |\n| **Injury of the lateral geniculate body** | The LGB is a **post-chiasmal** relay, so a lesion there gives a **contralateral homonymous hemianopia** — and because the LGB has a peculiar dual blood supply (anterior choroidal and posterior cerebral arteries), it can produce the two field defects considered pathognomonic of geniculate disease: the **congruous homonymous horizontal sectoranopia** and its complement, the **quadruple sectoranopia**. Either way, field loss is the entire clinical presentation; visual acuity and the pupils are typically normal. |\n| **Retinitis pigmentosa** | A rod-cone dystrophy that destroys the **mid-peripheral** rods first — hence **night blindness** as the presenting symptom and a **ring scotoma** that enlarges concentrically into **tunnel vision**, alongside bone-spicule pigmentation, arteriolar attenuation, a waxy pale disc and an extinguished ERG. The field is where the disease is measured. |\n\n**⚠️ Printed defect, kept exactly as set.** Option B prints **\"Injury of the lateral jeniculate ate body\"** — verified at high magnification. Two faults in four words: **\"geniculate\"** is misspelled *jeniculate*, and a stray word **\"ate\"** is set between it and *body*. The option is reproduced exactly as the bank printed it; the intended structure is unmistakably the **lateral geniculate body**, and no key is affected.\n\n**The trap being set.** Three options are diseases *of the visual pathway at three different levels* — nerve head, retina, geniculate — and the fourth is a **number**. A candidate scanning for \"which of these is not an eye disease\" will fail; the discriminator is whether the condition has, by definition, damaged the pathway.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: `op-glauc` — two of the four options are glaucoma-spectrum entries and the ocular hypertension definition is the answer.)*",
    objective:'Ocular hypertension is raised IOP with a normal disc and a normal field, by definition; chronic angle closure glaucoma, lateral geniculate injury and retinitis pigmentosa each produce a characteristic field defect.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.171, Q40 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` (ocular hypertension — raised IOP with normal nerve and normal field; the IOP/disc/field triad); `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` (chronic angle closure — cupping and field loss).'
  },

  {
    id:'opqb-t28-41', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-intro-exam',
    stem:'You cannot examine the retinal details by:',
    options:['Direct ophthalmoscopy','Indirect ophthalmoscopy','Retinoscopy','Slit lamp'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Retinoscopy is a REFRACTION technique, not a viewing technique. The examiner never looks at the retina during it — he looks at a reflex coming back out of the pupil and judges which way it moves.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` **p.34 §8** covers **direct ophthalmoscopy** as the method of examining the fundus. Retinoscopy belongs to a different part of the examination altogether: a streak or spot of light is swept across the pupil and the examiner watches the **red reflex** move **with** or **against** the sweep, neutralising it with lenses until the reflex fills the pupil and no longer moves. What the technique yields is a **refractive error in dioptres**, not an image of the retina — the fundus detail is deliberately defocused. It is invaluable in the **preverbal child, the illiterate patient and the uncooperative adult** because it needs no answers from the patient, and it is the objective starting point for a prescription; it says nothing about whether the macula is healthy. `opqb-t28-42` in this same exam turns on precisely that limitation — a retinoscopy result that is *emmetropic* while the cornea is astigmatic.\n\n**Why the other three DO show retinal detail:**\n\n| Option | What you get |\n|---|---|\n| **Direct ophthalmoscopy** | A **highly magnified (about ×15), upright, virtual, monocular** image of a **small field** (roughly 5–10°). It is the best view of the **optic disc and the macula** — disc margin, cup, rim colour, foveal reflex — and it is the method the cached chapter describes. Its weaknesses are the narrow field, no stereopsis, and no view of the far periphery. |\n| **Indirect ophthalmoscopy** | A **less magnified (about ×3–5), inverted, real, binocular** image of a **wide field**, seen through a condensing lens with a head-mounted light. Its **stereopsis, wide field and ability to see through hazy media** make it the method for the **peripheral retina** — the retinal breaks of a detachment, peripheral degenerations, and screening the premature infant. *(The optical comparison between direct and indirect ophthalmoscopy is **not taken from the course material** — the cached examination chapter describes direct ophthalmoscopy and does not set out indirect. The point that it is a fundus-viewing method, which is all the question needs, is standard.)* |\n| **Slit lamp** | Alone it is an anterior-segment instrument, but **with an auxiliary lens** — a **non-contact 78 D or 90 D**, or a **contact Goldmann three-mirror** — it gives a **stereoscopic, high-magnification, slit-illuminated** view of the retina, and it is the best method for **macular** assessment (macular oedema, epiretinal membrane, a full-thickness hole) precisely because the optical section shows depth. |\n\n**The distinction worth carrying.** Three of these four instruments **form an image of the fundus**; the fourth **measures how the eye focuses**. A useful reminder is that a patient with a dense cataract can still be retinoscoped after a fashion, and still cannot be examined for retinal detail by any of the three — media clarity limits viewing, not refracting.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Retinoscopy objectively measures refractive error from the movement of the red reflex and shows no retinal detail; direct and indirect ophthalmoscopy and the slit lamp with an auxiliary lens all image the fundus.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.172, Q41 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.34 §8 (direct ophthalmoscopy in the examination sequence); `L7,8) Refractive errors` (retinoscopy as the objective measure of refractive error).'
  },

  {
    id:'opqb-t28-42', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract-astig',
    stem:'If keratometry revealed a vertical corneal power of 46 diopters and a horizontal corneal power of 45 diopters while retinoscopy showed that the patient is emmetropic. The most probable diagnosis is:',
    options:['Irregular astigmatism','Diabetes mellitus','Lenticular astigmatism','Pseudophakia'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Two instruments disagree, and the disagreement IS the diagnosis. Keratometry measures only the CORNEA; retinoscopy measures the WHOLE EYE. If the cornea is astigmatic and the whole eye is not, something behind the cornea has cancelled it — and the only refracting surface behind the cornea is the LENS.**\n\n**Read the numbers.** Vertical **46 D**, horizontal **45 D** — the vertical meridian is the **steeper** by **1 dioptre**. That is **1 D of with-the-rule corneal astigmatism**, a real and measurable toricity. `L7,8) Refractive errors` sl.61 defines astigmatism as exactly this: *\"a difference in the refractive power at different meridians\"*, arising from a non-spherical **cornea (more common)** or **lens**.\n\n**Now read the retinoscopy.** **Emmetropic** means the eye as a whole brings parallel light to a single point on the retina — no sphere, and crucially **no cylinder**. So the eye's total astigmatism is zero while its cornea's is 1 D. The lens must be contributing **1 D of astigmatism in the opposite sense** — its horizontal meridian steeper by the same amount — neutralising the cornea exactly. That is **lenticular astigmatism**, and `L7,8` sl.64 lists it on the causes tree in as many words: **\"Lenticular Astigmatism (very rare)\"**. The rarity is why the bank can ask it: the *only* way to detect it is this mismatch between keratometry and refraction.\n\n**Why the other three fail:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Irregular astigmatism** | Irregular astigmatism has **no two clean orthogonal meridians** — keratometry cannot return a tidy 46/45 pair, and the mires would be **distorted rather than merely elliptical**. `L7,8` sl.64 puts its causes as **corneal scars and keratoconus**, both of which would also *worsen* the refraction, not cancel it. |\n| **Diabetes mellitus** | Diabetes shifts refraction by osmotic changes in the **lens**, and the classic story is a **transient myopic (or hyperopic) shift with fluctuating blood sugar** — a **spherical** change, and one that would make the retinoscopy *abnormal*, not emmetropic. It explains nothing about a toric cornea. |\n| **Pseudophakia** | An intraocular lens is a manufactured, essentially **spherical** implant; it does not generate 1 D of compensating cylinder in the exact opposite axis. In fact post-cataract surgery the usual finding is the reverse — corneal astigmatism left **uncorrected** by a spherical IOL, which is why toric IOLs exist. |\n\n**Why it matters beyond the exam.** This mismatch is the reason a surgeon **cannot plan astigmatic correction from keratometry alone**. If a 1 D corneal astigmatism is being neutralised by the patient's own lens and you then **remove that lens** at cataract surgery, the corneal astigmatism is **unmasked** and the patient wakes with a cylinder he never had. Comparing corneal astigmatism against total refractive astigmatism before surgery is what prevents that surprise.\n\n**⚠️ Printed as it appears.** The stem is a **sentence fragment** — a conditional clause closed by a full stop before *\"The most probable diagnosis is:\"* — and both numerals were verified on the page image at high magnification (**46** vertical, **45** horizontal, no sign or decimal printed with either). Reproduced exactly as the bank set it.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Keratometry measures corneal astigmatism only; when the cornea is toric but the eye refracts as emmetropic, the crystalline lens is supplying an equal and opposite cylinder — lenticular astigmatism, which is unmasked if the lens is removed.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.172, Q42 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `L7,8) Refractive errors` sl.61 (astigmatism — difference in power at different meridians, corneal or lenticular), sl.64 (causes tree: "Lenticular Astigmatism (very rare)"; irregular astigmatism from corneal scars and keratoconus).'
  },

  {
    id:'opqb-t28-43', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'All of the following statements about extraocular muscles are true except:',
    options:['The superiors are always intortors','They are supplied by the third, fourth and sixth cranial nerves','The inferiors are always depressors','They become stronger after resection'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The obliques do the opposite of what their names suggest in the vertical plane. The inferior RECTUS depresses, but the inferior OBLIQUE ELEVATES — so \"the inferiors are always depressors\" is false.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` **pp.15–16** prints the nerve supply and the action of each extraocular muscle. The vertical actions are the trap the question is built on:\n\n| Muscle | Primary action | Secondary actions |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Superior rectus** | **Elevation** | **Intorsion**, adduction |\n| **Inferior rectus** | **Depression** | **Extorsion**, adduction |\n| **Superior oblique** | **Depression** | **Intorsion**, abduction |\n| **Inferior oblique** | **Elevation** | **Extorsion**, abduction |\n\nRead the fourth row: the **inferior oblique is an ELEVATOR**. It is the muscle that lifts the adducted eye — which is why an inferior oblique **overaction** shows as the eye shooting up on adduction, and why a **superior oblique (IV) palsy** produces a hypertropia that worsens on adduction and on head tilt to the same side (**Parks three-step**). One inferior muscle depresses and the other elevates, so the word **always** breaks the statement.\n\n**Why the other three are true:**\n\n| Option | Why it holds |\n|---|---|\n| **A. The superiors are always intortors** | True, and it is the elegant half of the same table. **Superior rectus** intorts (as a secondary action alongside elevation) and **superior oblique** intorts (alongside depression). Both superiors intort; both inferiors extort. This is the pattern that keeps the eye's torsion balanced during elevation and depression, and its failure is why a IV palsy patient adopts a **compensatory head tilt away** from the affected side. *(That both superiors intort and both inferiors extort is the pattern of the book's own action table; the torsional interpretation of the compensatory head tilt is **not taken from the course material** — the cached chapter tabulates the actions without discussing head posture.)* |\n| **B. Supplied by the third, fourth and sixth cranial nerves** | True, and the book prints the distribution: **IV → superior oblique**, **VI → lateral rectus**, **III → everything else** (superior, inferior and medial recti, inferior oblique — plus levator palpebrae and, via its parasympathetic fibres, sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle). The mnemonic **LR6 SO4, all the rest 3** is the whole of option B. |\n| **D. They become stronger after resection** | True in the operative sense the question intends. **Resection** shortens a muscle and re-attaches it at its original insertion, increasing its resting tension and effective pull — it is the **strengthening** procedure. Its counterpart, **recession**, moves the insertion backwards and **weakens** the muscle. A squint is corrected by pairing them: recess the overacting muscle, resect its antagonist. |\n\n**How to hold the vertical actions without memorising the table.** The **recti** insert in front of the eye's centre of rotation and pull the eye the way they are named; the **obliques** pull from a point *behind* it (the superior oblique after turning through the **trochlea**), so their vertical effect is **inverted** — superior oblique depresses, inferior oblique elevates. Both obliques also **abduct**; both vertical recti **adduct**.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: `op-neuro-motil` — the table is the basis of every motility palsy.)*",
    objective:'Both superior extraocular muscles intort and both inferiors extort, but vertically the obliques invert their names — superior oblique depresses and inferior oblique elevates; supply is LR6 SO4 all-the-rest-3, and resection strengthens while recession weakens.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.172, Q43 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `ophthalmology.pdf` pp.15–16 (extraocular muscles — nerve supply and the primary/secondary action table).'
  },

  {
    id:'opqb-t28-44', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-va',
    stem:'You can have an idea about visual acuity by:',
    options:['Ishihara charts','Preferential looking','Retinoscopy','Maddox rod'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Three of these four measure something other than acuity. Only preferential looking measures how well the patient SEES.**\n\n**Why B is the answer.** **Preferential looking** exploits a behaviour every infant has: shown two fields side by side — one blank grey, one carrying a black-and-white grating of the same average luminance — a baby who can *resolve* the stripes will look at them, and one who cannot will look at neither. Narrow the stripes across a graded set of cards (**Teller acuity cards**) until the preference disappears, and the finest grating that still attracts the gaze gives a **resolution acuity**, convertible to a Snellen-equivalent. It needs **no reading, no speech and no cooperation beyond looking**, which is exactly why it exists: it is the way acuity is quantified in the **preverbal infant, the developmentally delayed and the non-communicative**, and the only option here that yields a number describing vision.\n\n*(**Preferential looking is not taken from the course material.** The cached squint chapter records it as absent in as many words — `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.15's own gap list reads *\"Preferential looking / paediatric visual acuity methods: absent\"* — and `L29) Sqint I` likewise notes **no preferential looking, no fixation behaviour**. The description above is standard paediatric ophthalmology, supplied because the bank offers the option and it is the correct one.)*\n\n**Why the other three are not acuity tests:**\n\n| Option | What it actually measures |\n|---|---|\n| **Ishihara charts** | **Colour vision**, specifically **red–green** discrimination. `ophthalmology.pdf` covers the **Ishihara pseudoisochromatic plates** in the examination chapter (**p.38**), where numbers or figures are hidden in dots of confusable hue. A patient with **6/6 acuity** can fail every plate (congenital dichromacy), and a patient with **6/60** from a dense cataract can pass them — the two are dissociated. Ishihara is used to detect **optic nerve disease** (acquired red–green loss in optic neuritis, precedes acuity loss) and congenital colour defects, never to grade sight. Its own limitation is that it needs enough acuity to see the plate at all. |\n| **Retinoscopy** | **Refractive error in dioptres**, read objectively off the movement of the **red reflex**. It tells you what lens the eye needs, not what the eye can see — a retina destroyed by macular disease still retinoscopes normally. `opqb-t28-41` in this exam makes the same point from the other side. |\n| **Maddox rod** | A **dissociation** test for **binocular alignment**. Its fused cylinders turn a spot of light into a **streak** perpendicular to the rods, so different images fall on the two eyes and fusion breaks; the displacement of the streak from the light then measures a **heterophoria** or **cyclotorsion** in prism dioptres or degrees. It says nothing about acuity in either eye. *(**Not taken from the course material** — `L29) Sqint I` records that the **Maddox rod is not mentioned**, and neither is the Maddox wing; `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.15 lists it among the tests absent entirely.)* |\n\n**The habit worth forming.** \"Vision\" in ophthalmology fragments into separate measurements — **acuity** (charts, preferential looking), **colour** (Ishihara), **field** (perimetry), **refraction** (retinoscopy), **alignment** (cover test, Maddox rod). Each instrument answers one question, and swapping one for another is the commonest examination error in a written paper.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: `op-intro-exam` — the question is a survey of what each instrument measures.)*",
    objective:'Preferential looking measures resolution acuity in a preverbal or non-communicative patient; Ishihara tests colour vision, retinoscopy measures refraction and the Maddox rod measures ocular alignment — none of them is an acuity test.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.172, Q44 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.38 (Ishihara pseudoisochromatic plates for colour vision); `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.15 gap list (preferential looking and Maddox rod recorded as absent from the chapter); `L29) Sqint I` (Maddox rod and preferential looking not mentioned); `L7,8) Refractive errors` (retinoscopy as an objective refraction).'
  },

  {
    id:'opqb-t28-45', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'A 70-year old woman complains of chronic tearing and discharge. Irrigation of the lower canaliculus produces a mucopurulent regurgitation from the upper canaliculus after distending the lacrimal sac. Which of the following statements is true?',
    options:['Jones one testing will reveal dye in the nose','There is probably a common canalicular obstruction','This condition is usually cured permanently by antibiotics','DCR is the treatment of choice'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Every word of the irrigation finding localises the block. Fluid entered the lower canaliculus, crossed the common canaliculus, DISTENDED THE SAC, and only then came back out of the upper canaliculus carrying mucopus. So everything above the sac is patent, and the obstruction is BELOW it — a nasolacrimal duct obstruction with a chronically infected sac.**\n\n**Reading the test.** In syringing, *where the fluid goes and when it refluxes* is the whole diagnosis:\n\n| Finding | Level of block |\n|---|---|\n| Fluid passes freely to the nose/throat | Patent system |\n| **Immediate** reflux from the **same** canaliculus, sac not distended | **Canalicular** obstruction on that side |\n| **Immediate** reflux from the **opposite** canaliculus, **sac not distended**, clear fluid | **Common canalicular** obstruction |\n| **Delayed** reflux from the opposite canaliculus **after the sac distends**, with **mucopus** | **Nasolacrimal duct** obstruction with a **mucocele / chronic dacryocystitis** — *this patient* |\n\nThe mucopus is the second clue and an independent one: **pus requires a reservoir**, and the only reservoir in the system is the sac. A sac that fills, holds stagnant fluid and grows organisms is a **mucocele**; when it flares it becomes an acute dacryocystitis with a tender red swelling **below the medial canthal tendon**.\n\n**Why D is true.** `ophthalmology.pdf` **p.67** gives **dacryocystorhinostomy (DCR)** as the treatment of nasolacrimal duct obstruction: a new passage is made from the **lacrimal sac directly into the nasal cavity through the lacrimal bone**, bypassing the blocked duct entirely — external or endonasal, with success rates around 90%. It is the definitive answer because it is the only option that addresses an **anatomical** obstruction anatomically. (In an adult, probing does not work as it does in a congenital case; and an acute dacryocystitis is settled with systemic antibiotics *first*, with the DCR done once quiet.)\n\n**Why the other three are false:**\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Jones one testing will reveal dye in the nose** | The opposite. `L9) lacrimal system.` sl.18–19: **Jones I** is the physiological test — fluorescein into the conjunctival sac, then look for dye in the nose after about 5 minutes. Dye present = a **patent, functioning** system. This patient's duct is blocked, so **Jones I is negative**; Jones II (irrigating after Jones I) is then the test that distinguishes a **functional** from an **anatomical** block, and in this woman it would confirm the anatomical one. *(The Jones tests are in the lecture cache; `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.4 does not print them.)* |\n| **B. There is probably a common canalicular obstruction** | Excluded **by the finding in the stem**. A common canalicular block refluxes **immediately and without distending the sac**, because the fluid never reaches it — and the reflux is **clear**, since there is no reservoir to hold pus. Here the sac **did** distend and the reflux **was** mucopurulent, which proves the common canaliculus is open. |\n| **C. Usually cured permanently by antibiotics** | Antibiotics treat the **infection**, not the **plumbing**. They will settle an acute flare, but the stagnant sac above an obstructed duct refills and reinfects — recurrence is the rule until the drainage is restored surgically. |\n\n**⚠️ Reprint with a changed word — recorded, not folded.** This question is in the corpus as `opqb-t4-148` with the same stem and the same option order, but option B there reads *\"There is probably **no** common canalicular obstruction\"* — **the negation is flipped**, which reverses that option's truth value (it is *true* in the corpus printing and *false* here) while the key stays **D** in both. D remains the best answer either way, but the two printings are not interchangeable and the difference should not be smoothed over in a fold.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Delayed mucopurulent reflux from the opposite canaliculus after the sac distends localises the block to the nasolacrimal duct with a mucocele; Jones I is negative, antibiotics do not cure it, and DCR is the definitive treatment.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.172, Q45 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.67 (dacryocystorhinostomy for nasolacrimal duct obstruction); `L9) lacrimal system.` sl.18–19 (Jones I and Jones II testing). Already in the corpus as `opqb-t4-148` with option B\'s negation flipped — recorded, not folded.'
  },

  {
    id:'opqb-t28-46', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'A patient complains of double vision when looking to the right. A probable diagnosis is:',
    options:['Right medial rectus paralysis','Left orbital floor fracture','Right recurrent pterygium','Right total third nerve palsy'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**⚠️ The bank keys A, and A cannot be right. The RIGHT MEDIAL RECTUS ADDUCTS the right eye, so it is used looking LEFT — paralysing it produces diplopia on LEFT gaze, not right. The key is recorded as printed and NOT moved; the same defect is already documented in the corpus at `opqb-t15-647`.**\n\n**The rule that settles it.** `ophthalmology.pdf` **pp.15–16** gives the actions: **medial rectus adducts**, **lateral rectus abducts**. Right gaze is therefore performed by the **right LATERAL rectus (VI)** together with the **left MEDIAL rectus (III)** — the yoke pair for dextroversion. **Diplopia is maximal in the direction of action of the paralysed muscle**, so double vision on looking right implicates one of those two muscles, or a mechanical restriction of the same movement. The right medial rectus is the muscle used in the *opposite* direction.\n\n**Option by option:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Right medial rectus paralysis — ⚠️ THE PRINTED KEY** | **Wrong direction.** It adducts the right eye, so its palsy gives an **exotropia of the right eye and diplopia on LEFT gaze**. Change one word — *lateral* for *medial* — and the option becomes the textbook answer: a **right sixth nerve palsy** is the commonest cause of horizontal diplopia worse on gaze to that side, worse for distance, with a compensatory **face turn to the right**. |\n| **B. Left orbital floor fracture** | Wrong plane and wrong side. A blowout fracture entraps the **inferior rectus / inferior oblique** and their fat in the maxillary sinus, giving **VERTICAL diplopia worst on UPGAZE**, with limited elevation of the left eye, enophthalmos, and infraorbital hypoaesthesia. Not a horizontal right-gaze problem. |\n| **C. Right recurrent pterygium** | **The most defensible option on the page.** A pterygium is nasal; a large **recurrent** one with **symblepharon** and subconjunctival fibrosis tethers the globe nasally and **mechanically restricts ABDUCTION of the right eye** — which is precisely the movement needed to look right. That is a recognised complication of recurrent pterygium and produces restrictive diplopia in right gaze, distinguishable from a nerve palsy by **forced duction testing**. On the material and on mechanics, C fits the stem better than A does. |\n| **D. Right total third nerve palsy** | Wrong on two counts. III supplies the **medial rectus**, so the deficit is again **adduction** — diplopia on **LEFT** gaze, with the eye resting **down and out**. And *total* means a **complete ptosis**, which covers the pupil and **abolishes the diplopia** altogether; a patient with a total III palsy typically complains of a drooping lid, not double vision, until the lid is lifted. |\n\n**The contradiction, recorded not disputed.** The corpus already holds this question as `opqb-t15-647` — same options, same key **A** — and that entry likewise records the key as defective on the basis of `ophthalmology.pdf` pp.15–16. Two independent printings of the same defect is evidence the bank set it this way deliberately or repeatedly, not a transcription slip. House rule stands: **`answer` stays at the printed A.** Answer the exam with A; understand that the muscle for right gaze is the right **lateral** rectus.\n\n**⚠️ Reprint — recorded, not folded.** `opqb-t15-647` prints the stem as *\"...complains of **sudden onset of** double vision when looking to the right\"*; this printing drops those three words. Same options, same key, same defect.\n\n**The habit that prevents the error.** Name the **direction of gaze**, then name the **two muscles that produce it**, then ask which one is failing. Never reason from the side of the symptom to the side of the muscle — half the muscles for any gaze are in the *other* eye.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: `op-squint` — the yoke-muscle logic is squint-chapter material.)*",
    objective:'Right gaze is produced by the right lateral rectus and the left medial rectus, so diplopia on right gaze implicates those or a mechanical restriction of abduction; the right medial rectus adducts and its palsy gives diplopia on LEFT gaze.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.172, Q46 (Grade Gain, "Final Exam"). Supporting: `ophthalmology.pdf` pp.15–16 (extraocular muscle actions and nerve supply — medial rectus adducts, lateral rectus abducts). Already in the corpus as `opqb-t15-647` (stem drops "sudden onset of"; same key, same recorded defect) — not folded.'
  }
